// `StackRef`, declared once for every `zena:error-stack` implementation.
//
// This library is not virtual, and that is the point. `Error` has a
// `StackRef | null` field, so if each implementation declared its own
// `distinct type` — they did, in three files, with identical text —
// `Error` would be a different type on every target, and `Error` is a
// prelude name and a well-known type. Every library that so much as
// mentions it would inherit that divergence, and no check result could
// be shared between two targets.
//
// What the implementations differ in is the two functions, which
// resolve by name through `Program.unitFor` rather than by type
// identity. See packages/zena-compiler/CONTEXT.md, "One Compiler,
// Several Targets", and docs/design/component-emission.md, Part 2.

/** Opaque host-side stack-trace handle. */
export distinct type StackRef = anyref;
